WEBVTT

00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,860
Hello, I’m going to give you a quick presentation of Jibian. First of all, we can see that on the

00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:09,540
right we have a menu here. This is used to go back directly, close all pages, and return

00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:15,060
to the main menu. This here is the terminal emulator. Here is where you can see all the software; we’ll

00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:19,280
see a bit later what it’s used for. This is the file manager, which lets you see all

00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:28,760
your files. Firefox is a web browser, Chromium as well, Thunderbird is an email client, Evolution

00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:36,080
too, and here we have LibreOffice. At the bottom left, we can see Applications; here all the applications

00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:40,820
are sorted and categorized. If you’re looking for a specific application, you can click

00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:46,160
right here on the small magnifying glass, which opens a search. For example, if we search for the terminal,

00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:51,380
hop, we find it. At the bottom right, we have other information, such as

00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:56,720
the two screens I have, the battery, whether your microphones and speakers are enabled,

00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:02,840
you can manage the power, see if you are connected to Wi-Fi, and the applications you are using. Here

00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:07,320
is the update indicator. You can see that I’m up to date, but if you’re not,

00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:11,360
you’ll have a big red warning panel, so you’ll know that you need to update.

00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:16,520
Here you can see how much the processor is being used; this is the task manager. Here is the

00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:22,140
date and time, and finally the user currently logged into the computer. Now I’m going to show you some

00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:27,640
useful applications. Here we can see the two email clients, Thunderbird and Evolution. They are

00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:31,860
more secure than webmail, and they are very practical because you can use them with

00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:37,080
PGP keys. Now let’s look at Software. This allows you to install all kinds of

00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:46,140
applications, games, and so on. For example, I’m going to install Lunanti. As you can see here, you can

00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:53,760
install it directly; you just need your user password. Once the game is installed,

00:01:53,760 --> 00:02:01,380
you just have to open it. Hop, you arrive at the test screen. I already played a demo,

00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:07,860
so just for information, I have a computer with 4 GB of RAM, and you can see that it works very well.

00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:19,200
I can build, I can break things. Or there’s also SuperTuxKart, a Mario Kart–style game but under a free license.

00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:27,180
Once the game is installed, you launch it and you’ll see that you can configure everything. So here,

00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:35,580
I’m going to play solo. You can choose your character, your difficulty level, do a normal race,

00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:42,000
your level, the number of laps, the number of opponent karts, the driving direction. Hop, you can start,

00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,500
and that’s it. And here, the game runs normally.

00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:04,500Now that we’ve had fun with games, you can see that it’s more professional. You have all the LibreOffice

00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:13,000
applications: classic LibreOffice, Calc, Draw, Impress,

00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:21,600
Math, and Printer. You can select everything. So here, for example, I choose something and you can see

00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:29,820
that it works the same way, but using free software. You can also see that there are other useful

00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:35,280
programs you can install. For example, in Development, I have Code OSS, which is a derivative of VS Code

00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:37,320
but in a free version. You can also do graphic design.

00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:46,260
In Multimedia, for example, I have Shotcut, which is the software I
use00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:48,820
to do video editing, including the video I’m currently creating.
